<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ban Zhao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 45–117</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Huiban, Venerable Madame Cao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ban Zhao was a Chinese historian, philosopher and politician of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> and the first known female Chinese historian. Daughter of the historian Ban Biao and sister of the historian Ban Gu and the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-chao">Ban Chao</a>, she was widowed young and devoted her life to scholarship. After Ban Gu died in prison in 92, she completed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a>, the history of the Western Han, supplying the missing Eight Tables. She also wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lessons-for-women">Lessons for Women</a>, a guide to women's conduct that was popular for centuries. She taught Empress Deng <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and the court, advised the empress during her regency, and as a court librarian enlarged the Biographies of Eminent Women by Liu Xiang.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ban-zhao.jpg" alt="Ban Zhao" width="330" height="520" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banzhao.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Han woman was expected to obey her father, then her husband, then her adult son; the historian <b>Ban Zhao</b> wrote in her Lessons for Women that a woman’s great virtues were respect and compliance.</blockquote><a href="/societies/han-china">Social Hierarchy of Han China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Eastern Han, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.66,112.4&amp;year=91" aria-label="Eastern Han on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7404 909 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ban Zhao"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7854 1179h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7854 1179h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.66,112.4&amp;year=91">What was here in 91 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
